.Dd October 27, 2026
.Dt TINTA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tinta
.Nd set the desktop's palette: its theme, dark or light
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Oo
.Cm dark | light | toggle | catppuccin | duck |
.Cm cyberpunk | phosphor | next | theme
.Oc
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl \-help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps one palette for the whole X desktop in a
property on the root window where every client can see it and be told
when it changes.
A palette is the dark or the light side of a theme, Catppuccin,
duck, cyberpunk or phosphor; the mode and the theme are set apart, each keeping the other.
Programs that follow it recolour themselves at once: nothing is
restarted, and
.Nm
tells no program anything, since setting the property is the whole signal.
.Pp
Every command that sets a palette also shows a desktop notification,
whose summary is the theme and the mode, such as
.Dq cyberpunk, dark ,
from the application
.Dq tinta .
It is a call to
.Li org.freedesktop.Notifications.Notify
on the session's D-Bus, so
.Xr awizo 1 ,
or any notification server, shows it.
The server's id is kept in the root window's
.Li _TINTA_NOTE
property and passed as the id to replace next time, so
.Ic tinta next
pressed four times shows one notification that changes, not four.
With no bus or no server nothing is shown; the palette is set anyway and
the exit status is the same.
.Nm
waits at most 500 ms for the bus.
.Pp
With no argument,
.Nm
prints
.Li dark
or
.Li light ,
whichever the palette is now.
A display without the property is dark.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm dark
Sets the theme's dark palette.
.It Cm light
Sets its light one.
.It Cm toggle
Sets the other mode, keeping the theme.
.It Cm catppuccin
Sets the Catppuccin theme, keeping the mode: Mocha's terminal
colours by night, with bright black lightened until it reads as text,
and Latte's by day, darkened until they read as text on its
background.
.It Cm duck
Sets the duck theme of
.Sy ladle ,
keeping the mode: a mallard's green and its bill's orange on a pale
green-grey by day, a pond's deep teal by night.
.It Cm cyberpunk
Sets the cyberpunk theme, keeping the mode: neon, hot pink above all,
on violet-black by night, and by day deep violet text on
hot-pink-tinted paper, with electric indigo for blue, teal for green,
lilac whites and a hot pink cursor and mark.
.It Cm phosphor
Sets the phosphor theme, keeping the mode: an old terminal's P1 green
on black with amber by night, its text a pale phosphor so that green
and red, a diff's lines, stand out; by day near-black green ink on a
teleprinter's green-barred fanfold paper, with a clear green and a
true red, the other colours vivid and their hues apart, with an amber
mark.
.It Cm next
Sets the next theme, keeping the mode: catppuccin, duck, cyberpunk,
phosphor, then catppuccin again.
.It Cm theme
Prints the theme's name,
one of
.Li catppuccin ,
.Li duck ,
.Li cyberpunk
or
.Li phosphor .
A palette set by something else, of no theme, counts as Catppuccin.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Prints a usage message and exits.
.El
.Pp
The palette is replaced in one request, so a program never reads half
of one, and read back before
.Nm
exits, so that when it has returned the change has been made.
.Sh THE PROPERTY
The palette is
.Li _TINTA
on the root window, of type
.Li CARDINAL ,
format 32, 22 items:
.Bl -column "2 to 21" -offset indent
.It 0 Ta the version, 1
.It 1 Ta the mode: 0 dark, 1 light
.It 2 to 21 Ta the 20 colours, each 0xRRGGBB
.El
.Pp
The colours are in this order: the eight ANSI colours (black, red,
green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, white), the same eight bright,
then the foreground, the background, the cursor and the mark, an
accent colour.
.Xr paleta 3
names them and turns the numbers into a palette and back.
.Pp
The property lives as long as the X server does: a window manager's
restart keeps it, a new login starts dark.
.Pp
A second property,
.Li _TINTA_NOTE ,
of type
.Li CARDINAL ,
format 32, one item, holds the id of the notification last shown.
It is no part of the palette, and no program but
.Nm
reads it.
.Sh FOLLOWING IT
A program reads
.Li _TINTA
at start, selects
.Dv PropertyChangeMask
on the root window, and reads it again on each
.Dv PropertyNotify
that names it.
Each chooses how closely to follow:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the colours themselves:
.Xr pt 1
and
.Xr rat 1
take their 16 colours, foreground, background and cursor from the
palette, and
.Xr mosaico 1
its bar and borders;
.It
the mode only, with colours of its own for each:
.Xr tiza 1 ,
whose drawing never changes colour, only how it is shown;
and the browsers and GTK programs, which
.Xr mosaico 1
tells through the dconf key
.Li org.gnome.desktop.interface color-scheme
and the desktop portal;
.It
not at all, when its
.Dv FollowTinta
constant is false.
.El
.Pp
A program run inside
.Xr pt 1
or
.Xr rat 1
that prints ANSI colours, such as
.Xr ls 1
or an editor, follows without knowing it: the terminal decides what
the colours look like.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev DISPLAY
The X server to set the palette on.
.It Ev DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
The session bus the notification is sent on.
If it is unset or names no bus, no notification is shown.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
A usage error: an argument other than those above, or more than
one.
.It 2
The display could not be opened, or the server did not keep the
palette.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Say which palette is set:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tinta
dark
.Ed
.Pp
Flip it from a key in
.Xr mosaico 1 ,
which binds Mod+Shift+t to exactly this:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinta toggle
.Ed
.Pp
Step through the themes, which
.Xr mosaico 1
binds to Mod+Shift+y:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinta next
.Ed
.Pp
Start every session light, in
.Pa ~/.xinitrc
before the window manager:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinta light
exec mosaico
.Ed
.Pp
Go dark in the evening, from a user
.Xr crontab 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
0 8 * * *  DISPLAY=:0 tinta light
0 19 * * * DISPLAY=:0 tinta dark
.Ed
.Pp
Under
.Xr mosaico 1
there is no need: it runs
.Ic tinta light
at sunrise and
.Ic tinta dark
at sunset, where
.Xr alba 1
says they are.
.Pp
Do something each time the palette changes, from a shell:
.Xr xprop 1
prints a line per change, and
.Nm
says which mode it is.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xprop -root -spy _TINTA | while read -r _; do
	echo "now $(tinta)"
done
.Ed
.Pp
Look at the numbers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tinta light; xprop -root _TINTA
_TINTA(CARDINAL) = 1, 1, 6053751, 13766457, ...
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to standard error, after
.Ql tinta: .
One about the display, such as
.Dq cannot connect to the X server named by DISPLAY ,
comes with exit status 2.
A
.Li _TINTA
property that is not a palette this
.Nm
can read (too short, another version, a mode that is neither dark nor
light, or not
.Li CARDINAL )
is reported, counts as dark, and is replaced by the next palette set.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr alba 1 ,
.Xr awizo 1 ,
.Xr mosaico 1 ,
.Xr pt 1 ,
.Xr rat 1 ,
.Xr tiza 1 ,
.Xr xprop 1 ,
.Xr paleta 3 ,
.Xr desktop 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The property follows the ICCCM's conventions for root window
properties; its name begins with an underscore because it is not one
of theirs.
Contrast is WCAG 2's ratio.
.Sh HISTORY
Written in 2026 for this desktop, so that light and dark could be
switched at the root rather than program by program.
It was Free Pascal on xwire; it is now raz, over Xlib (libX11) and rdbus, built
with
.Xr robbie 1
and the same in every word it prints and every byte it sets.
.Em Tinta
is ink, in Spanish.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa ~/.local/bin/tinta
the program, put there by
.Ic robbie install .
.It Pa ~/.raz/lib/Paleta.raz
a plain copy of the module, put there by the same, for a raz program to
import.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/man/man3/paleta.3
the module's manual.
.It Pa ~/r/tinta/src/Paleta.raz
the palettes and the property's layout.
.It Pa ~/r/tinta/src/TintaNotify.raz
the notification, over rdbus.
.El
.Sh CAVEATS
Only X clients that follow the property change: GTK and Qt programs
and browsers do not; see
.Pa README.md
for what does.  Run
.Ic robbie install
in
.Pa ~/r/tinta
to build and install it.
The choice is not remembered across logins; put
.Nm
in
.Pa ~/.xinitrc
for that.
.Pp
An X server with no clients left resets and forgets every property,
this one too; a running session always has its window manager, but a
bare
.Xr Xvfb 1
in a test needs
.Fl noreset .
